 3. 🗳️ Ex-aides bet against Harris run 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Photo: Kevin Lamarque/AFP via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Many of those who know Kamala Harris best think she ultimately won't run for the White House in 2028.

 Why it matters: What the former vice president decides to do is one of the biggest X factors in the next presidential contest. Early polls suggest Harris is the frontrunner to be the Democratic nominee, and if she opted out, it would scramble the primary, Axios' Holly Otterbein writes. 📝 We surveyed eight former Harris aides and advisers, including those who worked for her at a senior level, to get their most up-to-date read. Only one thought Harris probably will run. Two said it's a coin flip, and five said she likely won't. Here's why:

 She's doing "the bare minimum" to stay in the conversation, as one ex-aide put it. Former staffers argue that if she were gung-ho about running she'd be hiring more, traveling more and inserting herself into policy debates more. She's a rational actor. Former aides say Harris is well aware of the hurdles that would await her if she ran again. The money might not be there. Many high-dollar donors are still furious about Harris' loss and don't want her to run again. She isn't an egomaniac. Multiple people who worked for Harris say she lacks the "sociopathic" belief — their words, not ours — that it's she and she alone who should be president. Some tea leaves suggest she's lukewarm on running . Harris effectively gave up control of her @KamalaHQ social media account, and bought an $8 million house in Malibu with her husband. Read on ... Alex Thompson contributed reporting. 

 
 
