 5. 🎰 Betting turf war 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 DraftKings, FanDuel and a few smaller sportsbooks had largely cornered the sports gambling industry until prediction markets suddenly began threatening their turf.

 The fresh competition has triggered an explosion of shock marketing and promotions in the sports betting space, Axios' Nathan Bomey writes. 🎲 State of play: Traditional sportsbooks had about 76% of legal U.S. sports wagering in 2023, but that share fell to 64% in the first half of 2026, according to research by digital market intelligence firm Sensor Tower. Prediction markets went from 1% to 16% during that period.

 Ad spending by prediction markets and the major sportsbooks rose 21% in the first seven months of 2026, compared to the same period a year earlier, according to Nielsen research. The latest: Kalshi, Polymarket and upstarts like Novig have been signing celebrity sponsors and airing splashy ads this football season in a stark reflection of the intensifying competition for wagers.

