 8. 🏛️ 1 ancient thing: All roads lead to ... 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A street in Ostia, the ancient seaport of Rome. Photo: Tom Brughmans via Reuters The Roman Empire's roads formed the backbone of the world's first continental-scale transportation system.

 Now, researchers have mapped the network — 190,000 miles of routes crisscrossing Europe, the Middle East and North Africa — in unprecedented detail. Many modern highways trace these ancient roads. 🕌 The study showed the old adage "all roads lead to Rome" was not exactly true.

 The empire's later imperial capital, Constantinople — now Istanbul — was a better fit for that maxim. Go deeper . 

 
 
